To create a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text, you can use the `markdown-c` library. This library provides a straightforward way to convert Markdown to HTML.

First, install the `markdown-c` library using npm:

```sh
npm install markdown-c
```

Then, use the function in your C++ code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <markdown-c.hpp>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdownText) {
    markdown::Markdown md;
    std::string htmlText = md.generate(markdownText);
    return htmlText;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdownText = "# Hello, World!\nThis is a sample markdown text.";
    std::string htmlText = markdownToHtml(markdownText);
    std::cout << "Markdown Text: " << markdownText << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML Text: " << htmlText << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses the `markdown-c` library to convert the input markdown text to HTML. The `main` function demonstrates how to use the `markdownToHtml` function by printing both markdown and html text.

